{:edb/db-before-t 1173 :edb/db-after-t 1174 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534486 50 #inst "2026-10-07T08:12:29.820Z" 13194139534486 true] [17592186068532 1100 "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/0af8c0419c649a2986c7a4112399a532.png)" 13194139534486 false] [17592186068532 1100 "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b98474e6de2c94876a75758d56a7fb356de22bccd029bfeed0207affebab09ce.png)" 13194139534486 true] [17592186068532 1145 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1$. So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/e2dad27370be8b6a9b82b1adc02d06c5.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{1}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[(\\sqrt{x}) - (x^{2})]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x} - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 false] [17592186068532 1145 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/1b1c2b1826fa988ba23f7270b4c8bbdc91bf03066e8ca3638def329466d5b93e.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt{x})-({x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186068533 1100 "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/5c6f110a048606921590ea4e6403ede0.png)" 13194139534486 false] [17592186068533 1100 "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/01f139c53b6328341b463f5ad941d37fccb241fff109c0584207017341e8e5f7.png)" 13194139534486 true] [17592186068533 1145 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/88219aab4954daef055e1fa24e9e55b6.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2$.\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}[(2) - (x^{2} - 2x + 2)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-x^{2} + 2x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(2x - x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{3}) \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (2^{2} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2^{3}) - (0 - 0) \\\\\n&= (4 - \\frac{8}{3}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 false] [17592186068533 1145 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/39798ef4ff6e898527864628dc2750f88b115c03b2d6b8fb729f30b21d2d2bf5.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3})-(0-0) \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186068534 1100 "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9$?" 13194139534486 false] [17592186068534 1100 "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9?$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186068534 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5f6980e3a7e4c7d1a5cc5d9b93a0110764b34ea6b7a4bec7c828f04cef842f7f.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+5 & =x+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3c52f1405079380459e60ad35e05218d1f0468ee177f1e383498c672d48eaa8d.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(x+9)-({x}^{2}+x+5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186068534 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/36b16b895b0234f1f9e10a91977d6c66.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + x + 5 &= x + 9 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/c27aaed348fa7bebc01f25f85b3a720e.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{-2}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}[(x + 9) - (x^{2} + x + 5)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}(4 - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 false] [17592186068696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291496 13194139534486 true] [17592186068696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291503 13194139534486 true] [17592186068696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291531 13194139534486 true] [17592186068696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291558 13194139534486 true] [17592186068696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291565 13194139534486 true] [17592186068697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291489 13194139534486 true] [17592186068697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291524 13194139534486 true] [17592186068697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291538 13194139534486 true] [17592186068697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291545 13194139534486 true] [17592186068697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291548 13194139534486 true] [17592186068698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291482 13194139534486 true] [17592186068698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291510 13194139534486 true] [17592186068698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291517 13194139534486 true] [17592186068698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291551 13194139534486 true] [17592186069313 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186069313 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y = 4x^{2} - x$ and $y = 2 - x^{2} - 4x$?" 13194139534486 false] [17592186069313 1100 "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=4{x}^{2}-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x?$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186069313 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/913ec93445f3ed571fcf98ad762eba2a1bc657dce7442c8e48fc4d5f36817def.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =2-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-2)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/77493917a13269c78acef8ff6fe57b5f350020542805e316f82f7a17dabedd24.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(2-{x}^{2}-4x)-(4{x}^{2}-x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(-5{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291483 13194139534486 true] [17592186291482 1089 "q-11487" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186291482 1100 "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=4x?$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291482 1113 #uuid "dd8d0f5b-37a6-5193-9c93-2abd275a5141" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291482 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-5 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=5.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/bf79cec9c1ede4a95b3e20415fbf37224ca8616426e8530043fa4a4b54780bab.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(4x)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291484 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291485 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291486 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291487 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291488 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291485 13194139534486 true] [17592186291483 1110 #uuid "16e9d5af-1c25-5005-9231-15a7611d204a" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291484 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291484 1111 #uuid "cfac9120-eb2d-5507-b553-90da7e76cd63" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291485 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291485 1111 #uuid "15b73c36-3bdc-5603-8ee1-16a746763542" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291486 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291486 1111 #uuid "fc78b8a5-decd-5153-98a2-48f1e78f2d67" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291487 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291487 1111 #uuid "5ffc81ca-faa8-5d1c-bb8a-2c0898e4c2fe" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291488 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291488 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291488 1111 #uuid "24197187-5ffd-5de5-adcb-49c45ff22388" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291489 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291490 13194139534486 true] [17592186291489 1089 "q-11488" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291489 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534486 true] [17592186291489 1100 "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=3{x}^{2}.$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291489 1113 #uuid "d7acbd35-2c0b-57d1-af2a-99344b438f27" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291489 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/4e3758ddf652c3dd7df10d381b42ca1dde50d48250bed672b740329191202597.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/54ad3b4c8bc3aef707af764cd8d8badf9390737dc75491aa66ea554f531a7576.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(3{x}^{2})-(4{x}^{2}-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2((1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291491 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291492 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291493 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291494 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291495 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291492 13194139534486 true] [17592186291490 1110 #uuid "e5afbd31-dfe4-5306-a41f-19a0229cf16a" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291491 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291491 1111 #uuid "707b79b8-625e-5731-ac5e-d62db9e56406" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291492 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291492 1111 #uuid "9feb6521-4acd-5629-b935-c878f5ed613c" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291493 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291493 1111 #uuid "0f0603b0-b608-5b2d-a2e2-9263798a3e11" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291494 1034 "0" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291494 1111 #uuid "d890a635-43b5-5954-bc9c-0b6ecb2eaa4e" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291495 1034 "-\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291495 1111 #uuid "197a676d-a3fa-5e3f-ab32-4e6eabf55b56" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291496 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291497 13194139534486 true] [17592186291496 1089 "q-120871" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291496 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534486 true] [17592186291496 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/0ac836789132a294051f6cbd0756f26407d002fa83a278b3b8825cfdd9af0b81.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y={x}^{2}+3x-2$ and $y=x+1,$ shown above?" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291496 1113 #uuid "6c05958a-e92d-52eb-a776-84210c48e5f3" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291496 1145 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/299608a54c0b5f568f32619722dd678af1388065f017620071ffb0473a686fa3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-({x}^{2}+3x-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291498 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291499 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291500 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291501 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291502 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291500 13194139534486 true] [17592186291497 1110 #uuid "4fb38d7d-3f02-5a95-9252-236f17f