limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/226ade4a3b546484ba2e9ce5f266391fb51e9fe4b62e2e78d12200ac54ecea9a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=8{x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(1-2x)-(8{x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291505 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291506 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291507 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291508 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291509 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291509 13194139534486 true] [17592186291504 1110 #uuid "35da2a72-17f3-52f4-9887-3a1a23d8be77" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291505 1034 "{\\int }_{-1/4}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291505 1111 #uuid "c11c3bf8-312f-5a70-9233-c53d2aa0cf46" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291506 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}+x+5)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291506 1111 #uuid "1a332f33-85b0-5324-8301-b44ff93699c8" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291507 1034 "{\\int }_{-1/3}^{3/4}(-{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291507 1111 #uuid "0378c0f2-21d1-51ed-aaf0-ebd978ab2497" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291508 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(8{x}^{2}-x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291508 1111 #uuid "25877345-fe31-505f-9cb2-97dd7779a860" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291509 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291509 1111 #uuid "b30f0b69-53ad-5c42-bc9b-a047fcdeaf31" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291510 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291511 13194139534486 true] [17592186291510 1089 "q-121512" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291510 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186291510 1100 "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=3{x}^{2}-5x$ and $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x?$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291510 1113 #uuid "d3a05e1c-a69f-56e3-b571-e32c1197e412" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291510 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/813677aa1b12481e990058b129cb9ad24945a3dfb818afacfb2ecb720d6b81d3.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5x & =6-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 11}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/a8c538d5f40f68506588695aa04b38f095360b6f51369d8169099806a9678de9.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=3{x}^{2}-5x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(6-2{x}^{2}-4x)-(3{x}^{2}-5x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291512 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291513 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291514 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291515 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291516 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291513 13194139534486 true] [17592186291511 1110 #uuid "e70b1c5b-29e7-59b7-b280-86a6990dddb5" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291512 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+5x-6)\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291512 1111 #uuid "18287057-ea0a-518c-9691-c7d0408a9617" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291513 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291513 1111 #uuid "08d2a3d0-fefc-5d13-86e4-568953213391" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291514 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{1/5}({x}^{2}+x+3)\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291514 1111 #uuid "ebb25768-4002-514b-bbdf-1848601fdcc2" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291515 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(5{x}^{2}+x+4)\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291515 1111 #uuid "372b9448-e262-5710-a406-4a4fd99da531" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291516 1034 "{\\int }_{1/5}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}-x+2)\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291516 1111 #uuid "d6683605-25f4-534a-9e56-69e2d3a9e473" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291517 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291518 13194139534486 true] [17592186291517 1089 "q-121526" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291517 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186291517 1100 "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}+2x-9$ and $y=2x+7?$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291517 1113 #uuid "f1267085-eeb4-5b23-9c27-dc0dee8c5147" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291517 1145 "First, let's draw the graphs of the two functions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/2514c3e7bf5352dbe26dddc479f66042b3736b93f82b97a60850f768a89a4e17.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-9 & =2x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/560fb0937828d5afb5de9593280a1177773598c5d3534ee26318e0bc2788f5ab.png)\n\n \n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+2x-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2x+7.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(2x+7)-({x}^{2}+2x-9)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-{x}^{2}+16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291519 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291520 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291521 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291522 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291523 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291521 13194139534486 true] [17592186291518 1110 #uuid "6b339274-4f6a-5f2b-92df-78c9f65fe43b" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291519 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291519 1111 #uuid "2392df36-67f9-5e27-a0c6-6b0026c175f6" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291520 1034 "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16+4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291520 1111 #uuid "fae4c85b-01f7-585f-9179-04329a1fbb59" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291521 1034 "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291521 1111 #uuid "a3647819-fcc7-5680-861d-bd11d552a8a8" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291522 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291522 1111 #uuid "3f8bb39b-43c1-5fbd-a398-407183920383" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291523 1034 "{\\int }_{-4}^{1}({x}^{2}-4)\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291523 1111 #uuid "71d53f25-d523-54c4-a780-b7ab3274cfef" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291524 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291525 13194139534486 true] [17592186291524 1089 "q-121580" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291524 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186291524 1100 "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-9$ and $y=7.$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291524 1113 #uuid "49edfe25-0508-5110-9496-2725f5f95615" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291524 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/85961197a76cc38713537f80a9c97bb158247c71a8be4633c98245ea158634b4.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7543876e9138be135630313317e162c9b256713f2565b0cd3b5a24bb9ba9a3b3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=7.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(7)-({x}^{2}-9)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291526 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291527 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291528 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291529 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291530 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291529 13194139534486 true] [17592186291525 1110 #uuid "887f178a-f341-58f2-a1a5-1775c0807647" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291526 1034 "\\frac{64}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291526 1111 #uuid "f11d683b-c1a7-5ea2-a1b2-44e326c96f65" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291527 1034 "\\frac{250}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291527 1111 #uuid "599e765b-9113-5edf-a098-b794cd8acf05" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291528 1034 "\\frac{32}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291528 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291528 1111 #uuid "c05dfeb1-ce69-557d-b5e6-784020e18874" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291529 1034 "\\frac{256}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291529 1111 #uuid "32667257-77f9-5c35-9ad5-917ddd15e307" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291530 1034 "\\frac{157}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291530 1111 #uuid "dad44faf-cc92-54f9-a63d-8f7f63e83ce3" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291531 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291532 13194139534486 true] [17592186291531 1089 "q-3121" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291531 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534486 true] [17592186291531 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9a87ae2b230e66b685051ee7d13987f5f68690a07a67ade9e7af0eb870f6224f.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291531 1113 #uuid "c69be2ea-7b70-598d-bb9a-251384a82770" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291531 1145 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/e77170aac6d81b1cfd0e93804b0c59802f28089d24aeaff065da04cabc44c172.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291533 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291534 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291535 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291536 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291537 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291534 13194139534486 true] [17592186291532 1110 #uuid "3d27a92d-a819-5dbc-8fab-b0aa6bc376dc" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291533 1034 "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291533 1111 #uuid "f68fe51f-a4d9-5f8f-aa10-f2e8fa70fec9" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291534 1034 "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291534 1111 #uuid "3c29da1f-9850-5b4d-a908-1c5101e99420" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291535 1034 "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291535 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291535 1111 #uuid "3af74cc8-e984-5307-932a-d1e2de0ecbe2" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291536 1034 "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291536 1111 #uuid "4f880dfe-cf92-5630-a407-fb84613d0779" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291537 1034 "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291537 1111 #uuid "5b1ec612-898d-50c9-bb3c-0d699ac52d41" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291538 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291539 13194139534486 true] [17592186291538 1089 "q-3146" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291538 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534486 true] [17592186291538 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/c61d9140e5b8e57c8f75b532c6e26e22faa073e6631984a8f6a7b6f4280882e3.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region enclosed between $y={x}^{2}+4x$ and $y=x,$ shown above.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291538 1113 #uuid "5da2e5f1-1de9-5fc2-b99b-9668203d66cf" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291538 1145 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ x(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/6b2232d5a2853b22c731c317b365822e371f3d419bfcb3e528a07f211a372553.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+4x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(x)-({x}^{2}+4x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-3)^{2}] \\\\  & =(0)-(9-\\frac{27}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291540 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291541 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291542 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291543 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291544 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291540 13194139534486 true] [17592186291539 1110 #uuid "534945ed-8eee-5cf5-bf23-7e34d5e5fac3" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291540 1034 "\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291540 1111 #uuid "a09d3698-cee1-5a99-8f63-6d930b6b8555" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291541 1034 "\\frac{27}{2}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291541 1111 #uuid "66e4c855-4581-5a3a-91b4-ea2c82c9b481" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291542 1034 "9" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291542 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291542 1111 #uuid "49511dfd-2f1a-5934-9ab9-5d081ce4476d" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291543 1034 "-\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291543 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291543 1111 #uuid "9009f62a-8f9f-541f-aaa9-3096b237215e" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291544 1034 "6" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291544 1111 #uuid "a9694e31-220c-5016-a56c-a6d1ed8cbfa5" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291545 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291546 13194139534486 true] [17592186291545 1089 "q-346621" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291545 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186291545 1100 "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=11.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291545 1113 #uuid "bbf20fe3-aec0-56b8-9300-f9d1b858cbfb" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291545 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/d2398c3a8a04d005dd4d0c6e8b06046a6ca98aa86127204e7521341213dfb464.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & =11 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/caf1c4d5df49aa18ed85158615be19745abb7c078269dde35f4e57d57fae292a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=11.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(11)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291547 13194139534486 true] [17592186291546 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534486 true] [17592186291546 1041 "field-1" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291546 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291547 13194139534486 true] [17592186291546 1110 #uuid "0029ae7e-f00e-594c-aaab-b3d91b97df04" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291547 1034 "\\frac{256}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291547 1111 #uuid "b4d18c80-2a7e-5607-9570-a2b1ce6478ee" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291548 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291549 13194139534486 true] [17592186291548 1089 "q-351944" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291548 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186291548 1100 "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}+6$ and $y=10.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291548 1113 #uuid "b345d94e-b06b-524d-aa98-24568478ff38" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291548 1145 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5ba7f7b4022c35c4afe7d285c57f87d6fcebfbcd8373ddc530f849e29fbc22de.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3ff79baa7d3d934dd9815b398cbd9f56990011370408ddede51e3c4110b4978c.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+6,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=10.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(10)-({x}^{2}+6)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291549 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291550 13194139534486 true] [17592186291549 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534486 true] [17592186291549 1041 "field-1" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291549 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291550 13194139534486 true] [17592186291549 1110 #uuid "4d39581b-30e9-596a-af6d-072df58968c5" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291550 1034 "\\frac{32}{3}" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291550 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291550 1111 #uuid "bfed83e3-a255-594e-a670-b12bc21afbf5" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291551 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291552 13194139534486 true] [17592186291551 1089 "q-49800" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291551 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534486 true] [17592186291551 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b5e422ab61b4ebcacafcadc082a1b85ebb453a689654a9cef7bc7e7a3a4721a3.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2$ and $y=g(x)=3x-2$, shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291551 1113 #uuid "b895499b-b902-5259-aaab-68a4f2e466b5" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291551 1145 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3x+2 & =3x-2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7d96f51f01c2e52756fe53a6b3a11dd5c7c6dd9cb15ce674a706b442a3e6ad2b.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3x-2)-(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291553 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291554 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291555 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291556 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291557 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1041 "selection" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291556 13194139534486 true] [17592186291552 1110 #uuid "508d5843-753f-5e35-b011-e4f47b56bb91" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291553 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{4/3}(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291553 1111 #uuid "f60f4407-4c88-511d-891a-c3cffb94fb8a" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291554 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-6x+4)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291554 1111 #uuid "7f6fdf07-4470-58e0-83c6-bec3fccf1cde" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291555 1034 "{\\int }_{4/3}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534486 true] [17592186291555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534486 true] [17592186291555 1111 #uuid "92f35698-fbf6-554a-a6d4-e3192b4628c8"